  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe low that brought rain and wind for Thanksgiving has become\nstacked and slowly moving into the Northeast. A pocket of colder\nair behind the low moves in this afternoon and overnight with\nfavorable conditions for the development of lake effect\nprecipitation. Forecast soundings have lapse rates fairly steep\nso even though surface temperatures will be slightly above\nfreezing, rain should change to snow tonight. The precipitation\nwill be done for most areas by the time the changeover occurs\nwith the Southern Tier and northward having the best chance to\nsee some snow. Accumulations are not expected as the snow\nshowers off the Great Lakes will be light and ground\ntemperatures as well as surface temperatures will be\nabove freezing. \n\nBy mid day Saturday, the pocket of cold air vacates the region\nwith warm air advection getting going as a weak ridge of high\npressure builds in ahead of another trough digging into the\nGreat Lakes region. A surface low develops and deepens in the\nsouthern Ohio river valley Sunday as the trough becomes more\namplified and transitions to a more neutral tilt. Once again,\nthere is not any antecedent cold air in place so as the\nprecipitation moves in Sunday night, rainfall is expected region\nwide. This low has fast forward speed as the trough becomes\nnegatively tilted as it moves into the northeast, limiting the\nduration of the rainfall. A low level jet will also move through\njust ahead of this low but will not be as strong as this\nprevious low. Low level stability is also present in forecast\nsoundings but still 20 to 30 mph wind gusts are possible Sunday\nnight as the rain moves in.\n\nAs as been the case for most of the lows the last few weeks, the\ncold air behind the low does not arrive in time before the\nheavier precipitation moves out of the region. Rain changes of\nsnow during the day Monday behind the front, though by then,\nprecipitation likely will be showery and light. Set up is good\nfor a brief window of more organized lake effect snow late\nMonday into Tuesday morning before high pressure builds back in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTuesday through the end of the week, flow aloft becomes more\nzonal with the polar jet lifting to the north. Temperatures\nmoderate back into the upper 40s and 50s with mostly clear\nskies. Better low level moisture in place may help keep\novernight lows from getting as cold as they did last week with a\nsimilar set up.",
